.page ulimit 1 show or set resource limits

# SYNOPSIS
*ulimit* [*-SH*] [*-a* | *-c* | *-d* | *-f* | *-l* | *-m* | *-n* | *-s* | *-t* | *-u* | *-v*] [_LIMIT_]

# DESCRIPTION
`ulimit` is a gapsh builtin. It shows or sets the resource limits of the shell, which every program the shell starts inherits (fork copies them, exec keeps them). Each limit has a soft value, which the kernel enforces, and a hard value, the ceiling for the soft one.

Without a _LIMIT_ the soft value is printed (the hard one with `-H`). With one, both values are set (only the soft one with `-S`, only the hard one with `-H`). _LIMIT_ is a number, `unlimited`, `soft` or `hard`. Sizes are in units of 1024 bytes.

Anyone may raise the soft value up to the hard one or lower it, and may lower the hard value, but not raise it again: a hard limit that was lowered stays lowered for that process and everything it starts. Only root may raise a hard limit.

# OPTIONS
@ *-n*
    Open files. A call that needs a new descriptor fails with `EMFILE` once the process has this many (64 is the most the kernel has room for).
@ *-u*
    Processes of one user (root is exempt). `fork` fails with `EAGAIN` beyond this; a fork bomb stops there. The default is 128 of the 256 slots of the process table.
@ *-v*
    Address space in KiB: the sum of what the program has mapped. `mmap` and `brk` fail with `ENOMEM` beyond it, so a program that allocates until it is refused is refused instead of taking the machine's memory.
@ *-d*
    Writable private memory in KiB: the heap, anonymous maps and the program's data. Enforced like `-v`.
@ *-s*
    The stack, in KiB (at most 8192, the size of the stack region): a program that grows its stack past it gets `SIGSEGV`.
@ *-c*, *-f*, *-l*, *-m*, *-t*
    Core files (there are none), file size, locked memory, resident set size and CPU time: accepted and kept, not enforced.
@ *-a*
    Print every limit.
@ *-S*, *-H*
    Work on the soft or on the hard value.

# EXIT STATUS
0, or 1 when the limit could not be set (the message says why), or 2 for an unknown option.

# EXAMPLES
    ulimit -a
    ulimit -n 16              # at most 16 open files from here on
    ulimit -v 65536           # 64 MiB of address space
    (ulimit -u 20; ./stress)  # in a subshell: the shell is unchanged
    ulimit -Hn                # the hard limit on open files

# SEE ALSO
gapsh(1), gapsh-builtins(7), umask(1)
